INTRO=ps(
'Teams and organizations turn individual knowledge and contributions into coordinated action, innovation, and decisions. AI is expanding what individuals can produce, but improvements in individual productivity and output quality do not establish whether AI improves the collective processes through which people accomplish work together.',
'This research examines how AI changes collective work and how systems can support collaboration across science, entrepreneurship, and knowledge work. We contribute empirical models of collaboration, AI systems that scaffold interaction, methods for measuring behavior at scale, and evidence on how AI changes the diversity of scientific ideas and judgments.',
'We use AI in three complementary ways: as a phenomenon whose organizational consequences we examine, as an intervention for supporting collaboration, and as an instrument for measuring collective work. Field observation identifies the reasoning and coordination that collaboration requires; computational analysis makes selected processes measurable; system design translates empirical models into assistance that we study through experiments and field deployments.',
'The three research streams that follow explain how distributed expertise becomes useful, what makes teams effective, and how AI could change scientific knowledge production. Each project connects a concrete organizational problem to its methods, findings, and contribution, distinguishing established results from preliminary evidence in ongoing studies.')
OVERVIEW=ps(
('How distributed expertise becomes useful', 'Expertise distributed across a community becomes useful when people can identify what they need, recognize who can help, and interpret another person\'s experience in relation to their own problem. Our incubator study describes how coaches perform this interactional work and contributes a cognitive model of coaching. The subsequent AI system uses that model to help novices articulate reasoning and identify risks before meetings, while helping mentors prepare targeted assistance and revise the knowledge guiding the system. <b>Pages 3-4.</b>'),
('What makes teams effective', 'Coordination, knowledge integration, and team relationships unfold through sequences of contributions and responses. Our multimodal research develops theory-grounded measures of these processes, compares AI annotations with human judgments, and relates interaction patterns to collective outcomes. Studies of science teams, entrepreneurial pitching, and sports teams examine how members integrate contributions, respond to questions, and regroup after setbacks. The pitching study illustrates the complete progression from recorded interaction to interpretable behavioral measures and outcome analysis. <b>Pages 5-8.</b>'),
('How AI could change scientific knowledge production', 'Innovation depends on a range of ideas entering consideration and on independent perspectives shaping their evaluation. Our computational experiments compare human and AI-generated scientific proposals and reviews to examine diversity at both stages. The finding of double compression identifies a collection-level consequence that individual accuracy or quality measures can miss: AI outputs can cover familiar scientific territory while concentrating ideas and judgments around a narrower set of possibilities. <b>Page 9.</b>'),
'These streams connect the design of assistance with the processes it changes and the outcomes by which it is evaluated. Coaching research explains the work that support must scaffold; multimodal measurement makes selected interaction processes observable; experiments on scientific knowledge production examine consequences across collections of contributions. Together, they establish why evaluating AI in organizations requires attention to collective work.',
'The research draws on a sustained entrepreneurship-incubator partnership, behavioral recordings from real teams, and interdisciplinary collaborations across management, HCI, and engineering. External support includes $75,000 from Microsoft\'s AI and the New Future of Work program and recognition as a finalist in the Stanford HAI and Google DeepMind AI for Organizations Grand Challenge. A closing experience page describes the field and applied work supporting this research. <b>Page 10.</b>')
COACH=section('AI-coaching.md','Understanding coaching expertise')+ps(
'The cognitive model specifies how expert coaching makes knowledge visible, interpretable, and actionable across projects. This contribution provides a basis for designing support around the reasoning that collaboration requires, rather than assuming that access to peers or stored expertise is sufficient for learning. [1]',
'The evidence comes from one incubator and characterizes coaching practices rather than their causal effects on venture performance. Novice challenges were identified primarily through observation and coaches\' retrospective accounts, which limits conclusions about novices\' own interpretations. These boundaries distinguish the empirical account of coaching from the subsequent evaluation of an AI system grounded in that account.')
SYSTEM=ps(
'Preparing a coaching meeting requires novices to externalize reasoning that mentors cannot directly observe, while mentors must diagnose risks and plan assistance from sparse updates. We translated the empirical coaching model into a proactive AI system combining an LLM, explicit project and risk models, a reflective chatbot, coaching dashboards, and a mentor authoring interface. [2]',
'Novices answer questions about their projects, reflect on possible risks, and prioritize an agenda. Their responses populate dashboards that summarize context and suggest strategies aligned with mentor-specified goals. Mentors inspect responses, review selected and omitted risks, and plan the meeting; they can also revise risk definitions and project areas when the expert knowledge guiding the system needs adjustment. The figure shows how these activities connect before and during coaching.',
'We iterated prototypes with four mentors and four novices, then conducted an exploratory deployment with a different group of one mentor and 11 novices, each preparing for an actual meeting. Interviews, think-aloud sessions, surveys, recordings, and comparisons of articulated risks and plans showed how the system shaped preparation. Novices reported surfacing neglected risks and reconsidering tasks that did not address critical uncertainties; participants described coaching conversations as more focused, intentional, and in-depth.',
'The deployment also exposed constraints: incomplete input could produce misleading diagnoses and additional verification work, while expectations of immediate answers sometimes conflicted with reflective questioning. The contribution is an approach to grounding proactive assistance in inspectable, revisable expert knowledge. The exploratory findings concern preparation and coaching conversations; they do not establish long-term skill transfer or venture success.')
METHOD=ps(
'Team processes become visible through what members say, how others respond, and how their behavior changes over an interaction. Surveys and close observation offer valuable evidence, but continuously examining these processes across real work settings requires substantial interpretive labor. Our second stream develops AI-assisted methods for making selected interaction processes observable at scale. [3]',
'The methodological framework distinguishes scale, resolution, and temporal continuity. Scale supports comparison across teams and settings; resolution links organizational constructs to specific behaviors; temporal continuity preserves sequences through which coordination succeeds or breaks down. For example, an aggregate participation score cannot show whether a contribution was questioned, connected to another idea, or taken up in a subsequent decision.',
'The pipeline connects recorded language, vocal cues, and visible behavior to theory-grounded behavioral measures. Codebooks specify the construct, unit of analysis, and coding rules; multimodal AI produces structured annotations; human judgments provide a basis for assessing agreement and inspecting errors. Analyses then relate behavioral patterns to collective outcomes while retaining the context needed to interpret those relationships.',
'Validation is specific to the measure and setting. General video-understanding performance does not establish that an AI system measures knowledge integration, cohesion, or responsiveness accurately. The research therefore treats construct definition, annotation agreement, and relationships with outcomes as separate evidentiary questions. The perspective article articulates the measurement framework; the empirical projects on the following pages provide the setting-specific applications.',
'Across scientific, entrepreneurial, and sports settings, the program draws on more than 1,000 hours of recorded interaction. This resource includes 400 hours of science-team interaction and the 100 pitching episodes described in the entrepreneurship study. The availability of recordings is distinct from the amount annotated or the validity of any particular construct.',
'This work is supported by $75,000 from Microsoft\'s AI and the New Future of Work program for Building Behavioral Process Models from Real Teams with Multimodal AI. Evey Huang independently wrote the proposal, with Brian Uzzi as PI. The related TeamLens proposal was a finalist in the Stanford HAI and Google DeepMind AI for Organizations Grand Challenge.')
TEAMS=ps(
('Scientific teams: integrating contributions', 'Scientific collaboration requires members to evaluate and connect knowledge distributed across people and disciplines. The relevance of an idea may become apparent only when another member questions its assumptions, connects it to evidence, or extends it. We analyze 400 hours of recorded science-team interaction to examine these integration behaviors in the process of collective work. [4]'),
'Our analysis focuses on how members build on, connect, evaluate, and extend one another\'s contributions. This unit of analysis connects the content of an individual contribution to what the team subsequently does with it. It distinguishes a discussion containing many contributions from one in which members make those contributions usable in a shared line of reasoning.',
'Current findings indicate that effective collaboration depends on these forms of integration. The contribution is an empirical account of the interaction through which distributed knowledge becomes part of collective work, supported by methods for observing that interaction across a substantial behavioral record. The manuscript remains in preparation, and the findings describe observed relationships rather than effects of an AI intervention.',
('Sports teams: regrouping after setbacks', 'Professional volleyball creates a different coordination problem: teams must respond to setbacks and reestablish shared action with limited time between periods of play. We study team huddles to examine how regrouping is enacted through observable interaction and how these patterns relate to performance. [6]'),
'The study finds that consistency in how teams regroup following setbacks is associated with their overall performance. Focusing on repeated regrouping episodes makes a temporal property of teamwork available for analysis: how reliably a team reestablishes coordination across adverse moments. The finding concerns an association in observed teams; it does not establish that a particular regrouping routine causes better performance.',
'Together, these projects show why behavioral measurement must preserve the demands of the task. Scientific teams integrate contributions across an unfolding discussion, while sports teams coordinate interdependent action under immediate time pressure. Both require analysis of how members respond to one another, but knowledge integration and regrouping remain distinct constructs with different units of observation.')
PITCH=ps(
'Investors encounter entrepreneurial claims through a prepared presentation and the questions and answers that follow. Those exchanges require founders to clarify assumptions, substantiate claims, and respond to concerns that a presentation may leave unresolved. We study this interaction as catch and toss: the exchange through which investors probe a venture and entrepreneurs respond to evaluation. [5]',
'The study analyzes 100 Shark Tank episodes, each approximately 7-15 minutes long. A multimodal LLM annotates each episode under a versioned codebook specifying definitions, scales, rules, and examples for the constructs. Human annotation of ten randomly sampled videos provides a comparison for assessing agreement. Structured records retain time, speaker, turn function, and behavioral codes, connecting the prepared pitch to subsequent questions, answers, and decisions.',
'The resulting record contains 6,730 turns, including 1,516 questions, 1,668 answers, and 746 decisions. The timeline preserves which question an answer addresses, allowing us to examine responsiveness within an exchange. Answer annotations characterize substance, coherence, and checkable specifics; question annotations characterize the issue raised and how it is framed. These distinctions make the content of an exchange interpretable beyond how much each participant speaks.',
'The concept bottleneck model summarizes these annotations into named behavioral features and relates them to whether any investor makes an offer. It separates delivery and control variables from question characteristics, answers, investor reactions after answers, and rapport and repair. The model therefore connects an outcome prediction to interpretable aspects of the interaction. The accompanying figure shows the complete measurement record and model, including an example episode.',
'Current results indicate that how entrepreneurs respond to investors\' questions can tell us more about investment decisions than what they demonstrate in the prepared pitch. In the supplied analysis, adding answer features improves the ranking of funded versus unfunded pitches by 17 percentage points relative to the baseline; the combined interaction features improve it by 18 points. These are changes in the reported pairwise ranking measure, not changes in a founder\'s probability of receiving an offer.',
'The study contributes evidence that entrepreneurial evaluation unfolds through interaction, with answers revealing information beyond the prepared presentation. The results remain bounded by the recorded pitching setting, the operationalized constructs, and the observational design. An offer made during an episode is the measured outcome; it is distinct from completed investment or subsequent venture performance.')
SCIENCE=ps(
'Scientific innovation depends on researchers generating different possibilities and maintaining independent perspectives when evaluating which ideas to pursue. A collection of individually plausible AI outputs may still narrow the range of ideas or judgments available to a scientific community. Our third stream examines these collection-level consequences of AI in knowledge production. [7]',
'In an ongoing study, we designed an experiment and a set of metrics to compare thousands of proposals and expert reviews from real grant competitions with outputs generated by frontier AI models under the same criteria. The computational approach combines text embeddings, diversity and geometric-coverage measures, and topic analysis to examine how proposals and reviews are distributed, alongside comparisons that account for set size and writing style.',
'The preliminary findings identify double compression. During idea generation, AI-generated proposals cover scientific territory broadly similar to human proposals but concentrate more heavily around common ideas, leaving distinctive human ideas underrepresented. Coverage describes the territory reached by a collection; diversity captures how contributions are distributed within that territory. A collection can therefore retain broad coverage while providing fewer distinct directions for consideration.',
'During evaluation, AI-generated reviews produce more homogeneous judgments. The website\'s detailed account describes how AI panels can address much of the semantic territory of human reviews while offering more similar criticisms. This distinction matters because generating additional reviews does not necessarily add independent reasons for pursuing or questioning a proposal.',
'The computational experiments also examine batch generation, independent calls, and scientist-persona conditioning, including comparisons across Claude, Gemini, and GPT. The analyses distinguish patterns that persist across original and style-normalized text from effects that vary by model, prompting condition, and measure. Distinctiveness remains relative to the proposal set and computational representation; it does not establish scientific value or realized novelty.',
'These findings provide preliminary evidence that AI may narrow diversity at both the generation and evaluation stages of scientific knowledge production. The study compares human and AI work produced independently, so it does not establish the effects of scientists using AI assistance or an end-to-end funding process. Its contribution is an empirical basis for evaluating AI on the range of ideas and evaluative perspectives it contributes to collective decisions, alongside the quality of individual outputs.')
