LEARNING=ps(
('Learning to collaborate during complex work', 'Design, engineering, and research require people to articulate requests, seek and provide assistance, form relationships, and coordinate expertise as problems change. Our CSCW workshop proposal examines how organizational structures, routines, and technologies can scaffold the development of these capabilities within everyday work. [8]'),
'The central distinction is between access to collaboration and learning how to collaborate. A communication tool may connect colleagues without helping them recognize what support they need or formulate a request that another person can address. This work situates scaffolding within the learning environment, linking tools to work processes and social relationships. It contributes an interdisciplinary workshop proposal and research agenda, rather than findings from an intervention trial.',
('Deliberation as a learning process', 'In a university design course, we designed, implemented, and evaluated a deliberation-based learning environment for scoping open-ended political projects. The model combined iterative design with deliberation, supporting students as they selected issues, formed teams, and developed project proposals. [11]'),
'Students advanced their proposals through cycles of offering ideas, raising questions, suggesting improvements, planning tasks, seeking information, and revising the proposal. The contribution is an empirically grounded model of how deliberation can organize project scoping and learning. Related workshop work, Encouraging Disagreements to Integrate Diversity in Collective Intelligence, places disagreement and the integration of diverse perspectives within this line of inquiry. [14]',
('Coaching and equitable engagement in making', 'Our CHI workshop paper examines the gap between access to makerspaces and equitable engagement within them. Preliminary research developed a task model of novice makers\' challenges and coaches\' support strategies. The paper used that model to articulate a design approach for intelligent coaching support, motivated by the labor required to provide dedicated assistance. [13]'),
'These foundations connect the coaching studies to a broader concern with how people develop the capacity to participate in complex work. They explain why the design of support must address the reasoning, relationships, and practices that make available resources usable, alongside the resources themselves.')
TACIT=ps(
('Making literary judgments inspectable', 'Literary style provides an earlier setting for studying judgments people recognize but struggle to describe formally. With Sarah Sterman, Vivian Liu, and Eric Paulos, we conducted formative interviews with 14 participants, elicited crowdsourced comparisons, and developed a neural model of stylistic similarity. Comparisons and examples offered a way to represent tacit judgments without requiring people to define stylistic categories in advance. [9]'),
'The released dataset contains 66,061 cleaned judgments across 21,630 comparison triplets. The evaluated model used an earlier subset of 916 high-agreement triplets, with 92 disjoint test triplets; it achieved 67% accuracy on those test comparisons against a reported 55% baseline. Overall disagreement among raters remained substantial, so the model recovered a partial shared signal rather than a universal definition of style.',
'Explorer and Editor interfaces made the representation available for interpreting patterns within and across texts. In an exploratory study with six users, the visualizations prompted participants to articulate distinctions and question assumptions, although model and projection errors could create confusion. Some users found overall patterns more useful than exact positions or colors. The study demonstrates support for reflection and interpretation, without establishing improvements in writing quality or collaboration.',
('Preserving conversational context in recommendations', 'Emoji recommendations also depend on context that an isolated input can omit. In No More One Liners, we developed Reeboc, which uses the surrounding conversation to recommend emojis. A controlled mobile group-chat study found that participants preferred conversation-based suggestions and selected emojis more quickly than with recommendations based only on the current sentence. [12]'),
'The mechanism concerns what information remains available to the system: the preceding exchange can preserve topics and emotions that are absent from the latest message, including when the new message contains only emojis. This work contributes a contextual modeling approach and an evaluation of its use in communication, with findings bounded by the controlled setting.',
'Across these projects, computational representations make selected aspects of tacit judgment or conversational context available for use. Their value depends on how people interpret the representation and on the information it preserves or leaves out, a methodological concern carried into later work on expert coaching and organizational interaction.')
WORKFLOW=section('AI-business.md','The problem in practice')+section('AI-business.md','What the system makes inspectable')+section('AI-business.md','Keeping shared knowledge revisable')+ps(
'This applied project extends the concern with making distributed expertise useful into organizational workflows. As in the coaching system, an explicit representation guides AI behavior and provides a place for experts to revise the knowledge on which assistance depends. The platform and its design features are implemented work; their effects on organizational learning, output quality, and coordination have not been established through a dedicated empirical evaluation.',
'Industry collaborator: Novohaven Inc. <link href="https://github.com/eveyhuang/novohaven">System code</link>.')
HARM=section('double-compression.md','Whose judgments shape the response?')+ps(
'This published study complements the stream on scientific knowledge production by examining whose interpretations influence computational decisions. Diversity of input and influence over a response are distinct properties: a system can collect different experiences while assigning classification and action to a narrower set of actors. The review makes that distinction visible through the relationship between beneficiary needs, implemented features, and evaluation. [10]',
'The paper received an Honorable Mention at DIS 2024. Its contribution is an account of documented alignment between human-centered objectives and computational interventions, with implications for how systems distribute interpretive and decision authority.')
